As the former Q&A columnist for "C/C++ Users Journal," I feel at
least semi-qualified to comment here.

I don't agree with your connection between Usenet literacy and
program-building acumen. My position: Installing and building
NetHack source is manageable provided A) you are experienced with C
(or similar) builds, or B) your patched NetHack source builds
correctly. If both of these are false -- that is, if you are not an
experienced C builder and your build fails -- the result will
probably baffle you, regardless of your Usenet-posting skill.

If you scan the set of newsgroups on your news server, you'll likely
find many that cater to non-technical people. For example, I can
identify news groups my friends would probably like; and as each of
my friends is at least competent with computers and the Internet,
I'm confident all could figure out Usenet and a Usenet client. But I
can't imagine most of them ever properly configuring a C compiler
and associated build tools to create even Hello World.

C is a twitchy language to program right. But beyond that, it's a
twitchy language to *build* right. Your allegation that (in effect)
building NetHack is a turn-key operation is most true for a
programmer experienced in her build environment -- one who has
correctly built a variety of third-party source sets with the
environment, knows how to configure her environment, and has good
diagnostic sense when builds go wrong. I doubt that description well
matches most people who might wish to apply a patch to NetHack.

The allegation is true more generally provided the build does not go
wrong. But what if it does? What if the non-C-programmer's build
system is just a bit off, with some environment variable incorrectly
set, or some program component moved, or some magic include
directory missing, or the wrong compiler version, or the wrong
compiler, or no compiler? How does someone inexperienced with
building C programs interpret a C compiler's or C linker's
diagnostics, let alone fix the underlying cause? And worse: what if
the program builds but doesn't run correctly?

Several months ago as a test, I downloaded the NetHack 3.4.3 source
set and attempted to build it with and for Mac OS X. Because I play
the tiles game, I attempted to build the tiles version. That
required building the Qt library first. (If I'm remembering
correctly, the Qt-specific NetHack build references files generated
by the Qt library build.) And it required modifying some of the
NetHack source (makefiles and headers) to match my system layout.

I experienced quite a few build failures. None was large; but I bet
any one would have defeated most people unfamiliar with building C
programs.

After all of that, I had a NetHack version that would load the
startup screen but go no farther. After spending a while trying to
debug the program, I gave up, wiped it all, and reverted to the
binaries from the NetHack web site. (Because I'm not actually
interested in running a patched game or in source diving, I'm not
highly motivated to figure this out.)

I have what I think is an analogous experience to non-C-programmers
patching NetHack: me installing and running Hearse.

When I first installed and ran it, Hearse complained about a missing
Perl module. Now I do not program Perl. I had never consciously
installed a Perl module. I was a complete Perl newbie.

I don't remember if the run-time error directed me to a web site, or
if I searched on my own; but somehow I found the source repository
for the magic Perl module. And I figured out how to install that
module someplace other than where the installer wanted to put it.
(Just as with the NetHack build, the Perl build seems to assume each
builder wants to put the build result in the same place.)

Running Hearse again, I got the same kind of error for a different
missing module, or a missing entry point in a module. (I forget
which.) This lead to another round of module download and
installation. I think I went through this dance about eight times,
with each newly installed module requiring some other missing
module. (At one point I had a circular dependency that I could make
go away only by editing one of the modules.) In the end, with all of
the Perl modules installed, Hearse worked flawlessly.

I expect that an experienced Perl type would either A) already have
the Perl environment installed and configured, or B) have a sense
for module interdependencies without relying on diagnostic messages.
I got by only because I have experience installing and configuring
other programming/build environments, and could extrapolate from
that experience to what Perl was showing me. I don't know how
someone without my kind of experience would understand (let alone
solve) the Hearse problems without help -- and that's in spite of
the Perl diagnostics being much kinder and more helpful than typical
C diagnostics.

To close: as with so much else involving NetHack, we must guard
against assuming skill in one aspect translates to equivalent skill
in another; or that what we understand is equally understood or even
understandable by others.
